@Tutorial(time: 10) {
    @Intro(title: "Mock Your First Protocol") {
        In this tutorial you'll test a `Store` that depends on a `PricingService` — without writing a mock class by hand. You'll mark a protocol with `@Mockable`, let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it.

        Along the way you'll meet the two verbs that power every SwiftMocking test: **when** to arrange behavior, and **verify** to assert it happened.

        @Image(source: intro-first-mock, alt: "A test file beside a generated mock class, with one callout pointing to a when stub and another to a verify assertion.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Mark a protocol as mockable") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Every mock starts life as a plain protocol describing a dependency. The `@Mockable` macro expands that protocol into a ready-to-use mock class at compile time, so the protocol remains your single source of truth — when it changes, the mock changes with it.

            @Image(source: chapter-getting-started, alt: "A protocol declaration on the left transforming into a mock class card on the right, joined by an arrow.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Consider a store that needs prices. Pricing rules live behind a protocol so tests never depend on real pricing logic. Create `PricingService.swift` with the protocol:

                @Code(name: "PricingService.swift", file: "01-1-1-pricing-service.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Now make it mockable: import `SwiftMocking` and annotate the protocol with `@Mockable`:

                @Code(name: "PricingService.swift", file: "01-1-2-pricing-service.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                That single annotation is all the macro needs. It generates `MockPricingService` next to your protocol — the equivalent of writing this by hand:

                @Code(name: "MockPricingService.swift (generated)", file: "01-1-3-generated-mock.swift", reset: true)
            }

            @Step {
                Notice the generated class carries **two overloads** of `price(for:)`. The `ArgMatcher<String>` version returns an `Interaction` — calling it, as in `mock.price(for: .any)`, *describes* a call for stubbing or verification and never runs your code. The plain `String` version is the real protocol requirement: calling it records the invocation and returns the stubbed value. That duality is the whole trick — the same syntax describes a call and performs it.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Create the system under test") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With the mock generated, build the class you actually want to test. `Store` takes any `PricingService` — in production a real implementation, in tests the mock.

            @Image(source: intro-first-mock, alt: "A Store box receiving a PricingService dependency from above.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create `Store.swift`. The store registers items and asks the pricing service for each item's price:

                @Code(name: "Store.swift", file: "01-2-1-store.swift", reset: true)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Stub return values with when") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            An unstubbed method can't answer on its own. `when` arranges behavior: you describe the call with a matcher — here the literal `"apple"` — and declare what it returns.

            @Image(source: chapter-getting-started, alt: "A when statement routing two different item names to two different return values.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create `StoreTests.swift`, instantiate the mock, and stub a price for each item you'll register. Then drive the store and assert the stubbed prices flowed through:

                @Code(name: "StoreTests.swift", file: "01-3-1-store-tests.swift", reset: true)
            }

            @Step {
                Run the test. The store called `price(for:)` twice — once per registered item — and each call was answered by the matching stub. No real pricing logic was ever involved.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Verify the interaction") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Asserting output is half the story; interaction tests also assert *that a dependency was used, and how*. `verify` takes the same call description as `when` and asserts how often it happened.

            @Image(source: intro-verifying, alt: "A verify statement counting two recorded calls to the price method.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a verification to the test. The `.any` matcher matches every call regardless of the item, and `.called(2)` asserts exactly two invocations:

                @Code(name: "StoreTests.swift", file: "01-4-1-store-tests.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Run the test again — it still passes, and now it also fails loudly if the store ever stops consulting the pricing service.

                You've completed the full mocking loop: **generate** with `@Mockable`, **arrange** with `when`, **act** on your type, **assert** with `verify`.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What does applying `@Mockable` to a protocol do?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It adds default implementations to the protocol via an extension.

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    The mock is a separate class, and nothing is attached to the protocol itself.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                It generates a `Mock`-subclassing class that conforms to the protocol, with one overload for describing calls and one for performing them.

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    The generated class has an `ArgMatcher` overload returning an `Interaction`, plus the real protocol requirement that records invocations and returns stubs.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It registers the protocol with a runtime mock factory that creates mocks on demand.

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Everything here happens at compile time — there's no runtime registry.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Which pair of functions arranges a stub and then asserts it was used?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `stub` and `assert`

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Close vocabulary, but not the names SwiftMocking uses.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `given` and `check`

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    That's MockK's vocabulary. SwiftMocking borrows from Mockito instead.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                `when` and `verify`

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                `when(mock.price(for: "apple")).thenReturn(13)` arranges; `verify(mock.price(for: .any)).called(2)` asserts.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
